M.B. Rahmawati
Faculty of Medicine Diponegoro University, Neurology, Semarang,
Indonesia
Background
Incidence of multiple sclerosis has recently increased in developing
countries, including Indonesia. Currently FDA-approved drugs for
modifying disease are still expensive and not covered by insurance in
Indonesia. Therefore Azathioprine is promising as alternative treatment
to prevent and reduce relaps of multiple sclerosis in developing country.
Aim
To evaluate the use of azathioprine in multiple sclerosis and
review the relevant literature
Methods
Three patients treated with azathioprine 0.7 – 2.5 mg/kg per day
for 24 months were evaluated in a retrospective follow-up study.
Results
Two patients were free from relaps during two years treatment with
Azathioprine. One patient suffered from relaps after tapering off of
Azathioprine (after two years treatment). Side effects were few and
were well-tolerated. Hematologic proﬁle was unremarkedly inﬂuenced.
Conclusion
Azathioprine might be an effective and cheaper alternative in the
treatment of multiple sclerosis, especially in developing country.
Long-term use might be needed to prevent or minimize relaps of
multiple sclerosis, but its long-term toxicity remains unclear.
Keywords: Multiple sclerosis, Azathioprine, Relaps
