The role of autonomic nervous system in intractable hiccup: A
review
A. Idris-Agbabiaka a, A. Jha a, S. Sarkar a, M. Millaneise a, I. Agbabiaka b
aTexila American University, College of Medicine, East Bank Demerara,
Guyana
bMinistry of Health/Eye Unit, Dept. of Surgery, Dammam, Saudi Arabia
Hiccups are re ﬂexes characterized by the onset of sudden
diaphragmatic and intercostal muscle contraction immediately
followed by laryngeal closure leading to the production of a “hic”
sound. Hiccup is experienced a number of times through life, it ’ s
usually self-limiting and resolves on its own. However, when it
becomes intractable (lasting more than a month), it poses a serious
chronic ailment that causes exhaustion, dehydration, weight loss, poor
sleep and eating patterns. Intractable hiccup is a common complica-
tion of brain surgery but little is known about its pathophysiology and
exact cause, although a number of factors has been implicated with
the autonomic system playing a major role in one such involvement. A
case of intractable hiccup in Dammam, Saudi Arabia was explored. In
this study we reviewed the literature and found the role of the CNS
and PNS in the onset of hiccups as well as known treatment methods
for intractable hiccups and future prospects.
